<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The app's UI weight ramp — Mulish, shifted one step heavier than its own
    nominal weights.

    @font/mulish is the honest mapping: 400 is Regular, 700 is Bold. It is what a
    typographer would write and what anything needing the real face should use.
    This file is what the *theme* points at, and it deliberately lies about 400
    and 600:

        400 (normal)     -> Mulish Medium      (was Regular)
        500              -> Mulish SemiBold    (was Medium)
        600              -> Mulish SemiBold
        700 (bold)       -> Mulish Bold        (unchanged)

    Every TextView that never names a face lands on 400, and across ~320 of them
    Mulish Regular read thin and washed-out on phone screens — worst on the grey
    secondary lines, which are small and low-contrast to begin with. Shifting the
    default up one weight fixes all of them at once, with no layout edits and no
    risk of the sweep missing a screen.

    700 is left alone on purpose. Pushing bold any further would flatten it
    against the new default and the hierarchy would go with it; the point is to
    raise the floor, not to make everything the same weight.
-->
<font-family xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <font
        android:font="@font/mulish_medium"
        android:fontStyle="normal"
        android:fontWeight="400" />
    <font
        android:font="@font/mulish_semibold"
        android:fontStyle="normal"
        android:fontWeight="500" />
    <font
        android:font="@font/mulish_semibold"
        android:fontStyle="normal"
        android:fontWeight="600" />
    <font
        android:font="@font/mulish_bold"
        android:fontStyle="normal"
        android:fontWeight="700" />
</font-family>
